WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.750
A boatful of creepy creatures from RL Stine's
Goosebumps books are brought to life on the

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.470
big screen in the movie Goosebumps, which
stars Jack Black as the famous horror writer.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:15.509
I'm Jan and this is 10 things you probably
didn't know about Goosebumps.

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:20.880
To bring the movie's zombie-like Ghouls to
life, eight actors had custom-made flesh-coloured

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.020
prosthetics applied to their faces.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.910
The ghoulish face prosthetics were made from
medical-grade silicone.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.380
Each set of prosthetics took a whole day to
make, and the actors playing the Ghouls each

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.289
needed a new set applied every day.

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:40.050
As for the Ghouls' long fingers, 1500 of those
were made by hand, then hand-painted and brushed

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.530
with clear nail polish.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:46.730
Unlike the facial prosthetics, each prosthetic
finger could be used three times.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.920
From getting their prosthetics applied to
dressing in hand-tailored vintage clothes,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.320
it took each Ghoul three and a half hours
to get ready.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.149
Several of the actors playing the Ghouls already
had plenty of experience playing zombies as

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:02.800
they'd worked on the TV show The Walking Dead.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.659
If you're wondering what the hair department
used to make Professor Shock's hair stand

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:10.729
up on end, it was actually laundry detergent!

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.890
To bring to life Slappy the Dummy, RL Stine's
alter ego in the movie, a ventriloquist's

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:20.680
dummy was made by Californian effects firm
Ironhead Studio, whose creature and costume

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.560
design has featured in The Avengers, Godzilla,
and The Hunger Games: Mockingjay.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.259
Director Rob Letterman wanted Slappy's face
to resemble Jack Black in the movie as Black

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:32.490
plays RL Stine and also does Slappy's voice.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.189
On set, puppeteer Avery L Jones controlled
Slappy's movements, and scenes where Slappy

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:41.430
walks were later enhanced with computerised
effects.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:47.060
According to young stars Dylan Minnette, Odeya
Rush and Ryan Lee, the movie's creepiest creation

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:52.350
was the Clown, especially because the actor
playing him never broke character on set!

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:59.090
To turn performer Vivian Kyle into the Snake
Lady, it took around 3 hours, which included

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:04.619
applying face and chest prosthetics, airbrushing
her hands, and fitting her skin-tight metallic

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:05.619
dress.

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:10.330
The Snake Lady's eyes were made from polarised
sunglass lenses, and could be popped out easily

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.730
during breaks in filming.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:17.130
As for her wig, that took three people 90
hours to make.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.110
The Bog Monster, who's known as the Swamp
Monster in author RL Stine's books, was a

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.970
late addition to the movie.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:27.070
Creature Special Effects Designer Stephen
Prouty created the costume by hand-sewing

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.290
thread that's used for camouflage hunting
uniforms on to fabric netting.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.680
Actor Nate Andrade, who plays the Bog Monster,
is 6 foot 8 inches tall and, in real life,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.850
is actually married to Vivian Kyle who plays
The Snake Lady!

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:45.280
The Egyptian Priestess's eyebrows are made
up of hieroglyphics which spell the word “Goosebumps”.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.810
The ice-laden wigs worn by the movie's frozen
people were made by painting wigs with white

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.750
and clear caulking or sealant from a hardware
store.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.690
It took four weeks to create fourteen wigs
which matched the actors' own hairstyles.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.560
As for the icicles that hang from the frozen
people's faces, they were made using clear

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.010
acrylics and vacuum-formed plastic.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:10.780
Although the movie's werewolf is a computer-generated
creation, rather than a practical effect,

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.400
a stunt performer acted out some of its moves
to give the filmmakers a point of reference

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.310
for how the creature might act.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:22.730
According to visual effects supervisor Erik
Nordby, the most difficult creature to create

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:26.980
was an evil poodle, which only appears in
six shots in the movie.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.900
The reason it was so challenging is that it's
extremely tough to make its tightly coiled

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.870
fur look real in CGI.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.670
In fact, it took the visual effects team four
to five months to perfect its appearance.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:41.400
Now, let me know in the comments below, who
are your favourite characters in Goosebumps?

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.290
And which are your favourite books in the
series?

